[decoded] "During the day, sunlight travels through the Earth's atmosphere. As it passes through layers of air, molecules like nitrogen and oxygen scatter shorter wavelengths of light—primarily blue. This scattering effect is why the sky appears blue.\n\nAt sunset, sunlight travels a longer path through the atmosphere before reaching the ground. This extended path allows more blue light to scatter away, leaving the remaining light to be scattered by the atmosphere. The light that does reach us is predominantly red and orange. This phenomenon is known as Rayleigh scattering, which is why the sky is blue and the sun appears red at the horizon."
